#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046660} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415883} :activity/id #uuid "2c1f6cc5-5968-538f-9311-8522a9dd360b" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050198 :topic/id #uuid "35c03b2e-e860-5cb2-96d6-2e103a19283f" :topic/math-academy-id 788} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415883 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415882 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "01aac47c-bc41-58aa-a4b6-55ca3bbd11b0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$9$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415873} {:answer/id #uuid "2ef719c3-66a7-50b4-a2f7-e921efeb034a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$8$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415874} {:answer/id #uuid "4e4b0043-0c74-53a6-a993-6328a52b0af3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$14$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415875} {:answer/id #uuid "58d37435-1075-579f-a1f9-ce0f3925f1fb" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$11$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415876} {:answer/id #uuid "f1fee109-4588-538f-b649-9c3fd53fe5ef" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "$12$ minutes" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415878}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415873} :answer-field/id #uuid "0b712d39-ff45-54f8-b106-03be0d0cda60" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415879}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "979b0f60-49c9-5b48-86f8-6e501f45d940" :question/math-academy-id "q-95151" :question/problem "The average duration (in minutes) of the videos produced by a popular content creator is $35\\,\\text{min}.$ Also, $5%$ of their videos have a duration of less than $20$ minutes. Assuming that the duration of the videos is normally distributed, find the standard deviation of the duration times, rounded to the near minute.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the duration of a video. Then we have $X∼N(35,{\\sigma }^{2}),$ and we want to find the standard deviation $\\sigma .$ \n\nWe know that $P(X\\le 20)=0.05.$ Converting $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X\\le 20) & =0.05 \\\\ P(Z\\le \\frac{20-35}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ Φ(-\\frac{15}{\\sigma }) & =0.05 \\\\ -\\frac{15}{\\sigma } & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.05).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.05)=-1.6449.\n$$\n\nFinally then, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-\\frac{15}{\\sigma } & =-1.6449 \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sigma } & =0.1097 \\\\ \\sigma  & ≈9\\,\\text{minutes}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "08ba30f8-c04b-524e-80f7-0ec881803edf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415887}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415884 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302161 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ce3962b-9372-53e8-94dd-19aae1e9b687" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.51" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300674}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300674} :answer-field/id #uuid "752c40b7-22f4-5848-b34a-2e00b4e7dbd7" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186301589}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "6829b460-7921-509d-b187-5e5360672369" :question/math-academy-id "q-324314" :question/problem "The lengths of metal rods produced by a certain factory are normally distributed with a mean of $100$ centimeters and a standard deviation of $4$ centimeters. What is the probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm?$ **Round your answer to two decimal places**.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ | |  | | $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ | |  |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.0\\,$ | $\\,0.1587\\,$ | $\\,0.1562\\,$ | $\\,0.1539\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.9\\,$ | $\\,0.1841\\,$ | $\\,0.1814\\,$ | $\\,0.1788\\,$ |\n| $\\,-0.8\\,$ | $\\,0.2119\\,$ | $\\,0.2090\\,$ | $\\,0.2061\\,$ |\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.4\\,$ | $\\,0.6554\\,$ | $\\,0.6591\\,$ | $\\,0.6628\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.5\\,$ | $\\,0.6915\\,$ | $\\,0.6944\\,$ | $\\,0.6985\\,$ |\n| $\\,0.6\\,$ | $\\,0.7257\\,$ | $\\,0.7291\\,$ | $\\,0.7324\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm$ is approximately\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the rod length (in centimeters). Then we have $X∼N(100,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(96.8\\le X<102.4).$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(96.8\\le X<102.4) & =P(\\frac{96.8-100}{4}\\le Z<\\frac{102.4-100}{4}) \\\\  & =P(-0.80\\le Z<0.60)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we express the required probability in terms of $Φ(z):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.80\\le Z<0.60) & =P(Z<0.60)-P(Z<-0.80) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le 0.60)-P(Z\\le -0.80) \\\\  & =Φ(0.60)-Φ(-0.80)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(0.60) & =0.7257 \\\\ Φ(-0.80) & =0.2119.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(-0.80<Z<0.60) & =Φ(0.60)-Φ(-0.80) \\\\  & =0.7257-0.2119 \\\\  & =0.5138 \\\\  & ≈0.51\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected rod is longer or equal to $96.8\\,cm$ but shorter than $102.4\\,cm$ is approximately $0.51.$"} :step/id #uuid "13582afe-72fb-5625-8464-5f459b09da11" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415886}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415885 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415881 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "83221c93-603d-5671-86b0-b2c0e8b223f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.22" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415877}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415877} :answer-field/id #uuid "3ee986dc-58c5-5a6b-9a45-c8daf6a2b863" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415880}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "384a66a5-a5c9-58f8-aa5d-723b50b59cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-325130" :question/problem "The fasting glucose level of a clinic’s patients is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and a standard deviation of $15\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Additionally, $60%$ of the patients have a glucose level between $0.92\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}$ and $1.08\\mu \\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ Find the percentage of patients with a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$ **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.75\\,$ | $\\,0.80\\,$ | $\\,0.85\\,$ | $\\,0.90\\,$ | $\\,0.95\\,$ | $\\,0.99\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,0.6745\\,$ | $\\,0.8416\\,$ | $\\,1.0364\\,$ | $\\,1.2816\\,$ | $\\,1.6449\\,$ | $\\,2.3263\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.04\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.9\\,$ | $\\,0.0262\\,$ | $\\,0.0256\\,$ | $\\,0.0250\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.8\\,$ | $\\,0.0329\\,$ | $\\,0.0322\\,$ | $\\,0.0314\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.7\\,$ | $\\,0.0409\\,$ | $\\,0.0401\\,$ | $\\,0.0392\\,$ |\n\nApproximately\n{{field-1}}$%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the fasting glucose level of a randomly selected patient. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{15}^{2}),$ and we want to find $P(X<130).$ But first, we need to find the mean $\\mu .$ \n\nWe know that $P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu )=0.60.$ First, we convert this to a cumulative probability:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(0.92\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ 2P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.60 \\\\ P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.30 \\\\ P(X<\\mu )+P(\\mu <X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.5+0.30 \\\\ P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring. We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<1.08\\mu ) & =0.80 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15}) & =0.80 \\\\ \\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.80).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.80)=0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{0.08\\mu }{15} & =0.8416 \\\\ 0.08\\mu  & =12.624 \\\\ \\mu  & =\\frac{12.624}{0.08} \\\\  & ≈157.80.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<130)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<130) & =P(Z<\\frac{130-157.80}{15}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z<-1.85) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.85) \\\\  & =0.0322 \\\\  & ≈3.22%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $3.22%$ of the patients have a fasting glucose lower than $130\\,\\text{mg/dL}.$"} :step/id #uuid "4bf19974-65f5-541f-96e4-687af8d2f431"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415886 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186302164 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fb6f7f4-57a5-52f7-8e64-395b91ab0e51" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "0.10" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186300689}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186300689} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc161ec3-c1d9-5eb1-baaa-2ab67d18a5ac" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186302031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "aace1ce7-1675-5a50-8ee4-f15b9647304c" :question/math-academy-id "q-324349" :question/problem "The flight times of a particular drone model are normally distributed with a mean of $35$ minutes and a standard deviation of $4$ minutes. What is the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge? **Round your answer to two decimal places.**\n\n*Hint: A table of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ is given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.06\\,$ | $\\,0.07\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,1.1\\,$ | $\\,0.8749\\,$ | $\\,0.8770\\,$ | $\\,0.8790\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.8944\\,$ | $\\,0.8962\\,$ | $\\,0.8980\\,$ |\n| $\\,1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.9115\\,$ | $\\,0.9131\\,$ | $\\,0.9147\\,$ |\n\nThe probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the drone flight time (in minutes). Then we have $X∼N(35,{4}^{2}).$\n\nWe want to find $P(X>40.08).$ Thus, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X>40.08) & =P(Z>\\frac{40.08-35}{4}) \\\\  & =P(Z>1.27) \\\\  & =1-P(Z\\le 1.27) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1.27)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the $z$-table, we see that\n\n$$\n1-Φ(1.27)=1-0.8980≈0.10.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability that a randomly selected drone flies more than $40.08$ minutes on a single charge is approximately $0.10.$"} :step/id #uuid "b1290537-191a-5941-898f-4165f87ba9c8" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415885}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415887 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186268925 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "209a5dc2-51b8-5cf8-a3fd-432567049203" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.1%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266433} {:answer/id #uuid "285a1d46-eca2-5392-87b2-9ae6a067d406" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "7.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266502} {:answer/id #uuid "383eb090-4034-58c4-90ea-549e44d474a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "11.5%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186266633} {:answer/id #uuid "7be87196-7809-5fb8-a83b-62481b462d15" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "9.6%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267243} {:answer/id #uuid "bc16961d-f939-5b46-a020-4871a6a9d710" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "8.9%" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186267828}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186266502} :answer-field/id #uuid "0642bb25-91a1-5589-b640-3a5b72872474" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186268415}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "763a853d-691e-5772-a9d4-a4d4d0b3eb60" :question/math-academy-id "q-133053" :question/problem "The diameter of the kibble of a particular cat food brand is normally distributed with a mean of $\\mu $ and a standard deviation of $0.7\\,\\text{mm}.$ Additionally, the diameter of $20%$ of kibbles is less than $4.4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter. Find the percentage of kibbles whose diameter is less than $4\\,\\text{mm},$ rounded to $1$ decimal place.\n\n*Hint: A table of values of ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ for different values of $p$ is given below, where $p=Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n| $p$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.03\\,$ | $\\,0.05\\,$ | $\\,0.10\\,$ | $\\,0.15\\,$ | $\\,0.20\\,$ | $\\,0.25\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${Φ}^{-1}(p)$ | $\\,-2.3263\\,$ | $\\,-1.8808\\,$ | $\\,-1.6449\\,$ | $\\,-1.2816\\,$ | $\\,-1.0364\\,$ | $\\,-0.8416\\,$ | $\\,-0.6745\\,$ |\n\n$$\n\n$$\n\n*In addition, you're given the following table of values for $p=Φ(z)$*.\n\n| $z$ | $\\,0.00\\,$ | $\\,0.01\\,$ | $\\,0.02\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\,-1.4\\,$ | $\\,0.0808\\,$ | $\\,0.0793\\,$ | $\\,0.0778\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.3\\,$ | $\\,0.0968\\,$ | $\\,0.0951\\,$ | $\\,0.0934\\,$ |\n| $\\,-1.2\\,$ | $\\,0.1151\\,$ | $\\,0.1131\\,$ | $\\,0.1112\\,$ |" :question/worked-solution "Let $X$ represent the diameter of a randomly selected kibble. Then we have $X∼N(\\mu ,{0.7}^{2}),$ and we want to find the $P(X<4).$\n\nWe know that $P(X<4.4)=0.20.$ First, we convert $X$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4.4) & =0.20 \\\\ P(Z<\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ Φ(\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7}) & =0.20 \\\\ \\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & ={Φ}^{-1}(0.20)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the table, \n\n$$\n{Φ}^{-1}(0.20)≈-0.8416.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{4.4-\\mu }{0.7} & =-0.8416 \\\\ 4.4-\\mu  & =-0.58912 \\\\ \\mu  & =4.98912\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can solve for $P(X<4)$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X<4) & =P(X\\le 4) \\\\  & =P(Z\\le \\frac{4-4.98912}{0.7}) \\\\  & ≈P(Z\\le -1.41) \\\\  & =0.0793 \\\\  & ≈7.9%\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, approximately $7.9%$ of kibbles are less than $4\\,\\text{mm}$ in diameter."} :step/id #uuid "cd1c27a9-4fc5-505a-a0b1-c103e40a7537" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186415884}}] :activity/title "Modeling With the Normal Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186415872}]}
